 By Neil Irwin and Courtenay Brown &middot; Aug 19, 2026 
 
 
 Bessent to the rescue! The bond market is reacting sharply to news that the Treasury will step up purchases of long-term government debt — a move that could ease the painful (and politically important) rise in borrowing costs.

 More below, including the awkward Kevin Warsh dynamic lurking beneath it all. 🇨🇦 Situational awareness: Canada has three days to lock in a deal with the White House before 50% tariffs hit billions of dollars of its goods after the Trump administration granted a last-minute reprieve last night.

 Today's newsletter, edited by Jeffrey Cane and copy edited by Katie Lewis, is 995 words, a 4-minute read. 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Washington fights rate surge 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: FactSet; Chart: Matt Phillips/Axios Washington is mounting its biggest effort yet to fight the rise in borrowing costs that are hitting homebuyers, businesses and the government's own finances.

 What's new: Treasury said this morning that it would put more muscle behind an effort to steady the long-term bond market — a surprise announcement that immediately drove borrowing costs down. Why it matters: The timing underscores how central lower interest rates have become to the Trump administration's definition of a strong economy.

 Consider how the run-up in yields has compounded the affordability squeeze heading into the midterms. Elevated rates were keeping mortgages and other loans painfully expensive alongside the broader inflationary fallout from the Iran war. What they're saying: "This is effectively the equivalent of verbal intervention from the U.S. Treasury," Gennadiy Goldberg, head of U.S. rates strategy at TD Securities, tells Axios.

 He calls it "Treasury firing a warning shot across the market's bow rather than doing something just yet," noting that the larger purchases don't begin for another few weeks. Details: The Treasury Department is increasing its purchases of long-term government debt — a mechanism that should boost demand for bonds at a time when investors have been selling them (and, in turn, pushing yields higher).

 Treasury will raise the maximum size of each purchase to at least $4 billion, from $2 billion now, focusing on longer-term Treasuries in the 10- to 30-year range — exactly where yields have been under the most pressure. The expansion will run from Sept. 9 through Nov. 4, when Treasury conducts its next regular review of its borrowing plans — a schedule that also notably overlaps with the final stretch of the midterm campaign. " This increase in buyback operation sizes reflects Treasury's desire to provide greater liquidity support in longer-dated nominal sectors where there is consistent strong sponsorship from market participants," the Treasury Department said in a statement.

 Treasury says investors have routinely offered it more long-term bonds than it has been willing to buy, giving it room to expand the program. By the numbers: The knee-jerk reaction was swift. 

 The yield on the 30-year Treasury bond fell as much as 0.1 percentage point following the announcement, an unusually sharp move so quickly. But it barely dents the run-up of recent weeks. Long-term borrowing costs remain near multidecade highs. The 30-year yield is around 5.2% this morning, up from roughly 4.63% just before the Iran war. The big picture: Treasury's purchases are tiny relative to the roughly $30 trillion U.S. government debt market. In other words, the purchases alone will not determine where long-term interest rates go.

 The symbolism is what matters — that Treasury is showing it's willing to use the tools it has to ease some of the pressure in a market that has been bleeding for weeks. Yes, but: It's unclear how long the reprieve will last. The last time the administration tried to use the government's balance sheet to push borrowing costs lower offered only fleeting relief.

 Housing chief Bill Pulte this year championed purchases of mortgage bonds by Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac to help bring down mortgage rates. The announcement initially pushed mortgage rates lower, but the relief proved fleeting. Rates have since climbed back up. Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent has shown willingness to step into markets when prices move in an unwelcome direction.

 Treasury intervened to prop up the Japanese yen earlier this month, producing an immediate market reaction that later faded. That intervention also reduced the risk that Japan would need to sell U.S. Treasuries to raise dollars to support its currency. The bottom line: Washington is showing it won't sit by as high borrowing costs become an economic and political liability.

 
 
